Thank you for providing the CodeSandbox link. I've taken a look at the code, and I believe I see the issue.

The problem is that the `useTrackedState` hook provided by `react-tracked` is not designed to handle async updates to the state. In your code, you're fetching data using `react-query` and updating the `react-tracked` state with the new data. However, if the `useTrackedState` hook is called during the time when the data is being fetched and updated, it will return the old state, which can cause duplicate items to appear in the UI.

To fix this, you can modify your code to use the `useTrackedState` hook inside a `useEffect` hook that only runs when the data fetching is complete. Here's an example of how you could modify the `App` component to fix the issue:
```jsx
function App() {
  const { status, data, error, isFetching } = useQuery("posts", fetchPosts);

  useEffect(() => {
    // Update the tracked state only when the data fetching is complete
    if (status === "success") {
      setPosts(data);
    }
  }, [status, data]);

  const [posts, setPosts] = useTrackedState(postsAtom);

  if (status === "loading") return <div>Loading...</div>;

  if (status === "error") return <div>Error: {error.message}</div>;

  return (
    <div>
      {isFetching && <div>Updating...</div>}
      <SuspenseList revealOrder="forwards">
        {posts.map((post) => (
          <Suspense key={post.id} fallback={<div>Loading post...</div>}>
            <Post post={post} />
          </Suspense>
        ))}
      </SuspenseList>
    </div>
  );
}
```
Here, we're using the `useEffect` hook to update the tracked state only when the data fetching is complete. This ensures that the `useTrackedState` hook is always returning the latest state.

