To implement a fully transparent proxy server using the `http-proxy` module in Node.js, follow these steps:

1. First, make sure you have Node.js installed on your system. You can download and install Node.js from the official website: <https://nodejs.org/en/download/>
2. Next, create a new directory for your proxy server project, and navigate to it in your terminal or command prompt.
3. Initialize a new Node.js project by running `npm init -y`. This will create a `package.json` file with default values.
4. Install the `http-proxy` module by running `npm install http-proxy`.
5. Create a new file called `proxy-server.js` in your project directory and add the following code:
```javascript
const http = require('http');
const httpProxy = require('http-proxy');

const proxy = httpProxy.createProxyServer({});

const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  // Obtain the target URL from the request's headers
  const targetUrl = req.headers['target-url'];

  // Check if the target URL is provided
  if (!targetUrl) {
    res.writeHead(400, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('Error: Missing target URL in headers');
    return;
  }

  // Forward the request to the target URL
  proxy.web(req, res, { target: targetUrl }, (error) => {
    res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end(`Error: ${error.message}`);
  });
});

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 8080;

server.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Proxy server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```
This code sets up a simple HTTP server that listens for incoming requests, and uses the `http-proxy` module to forward the requests to the target URL specified in the request headers. It doesn't modify the headers in any way, ensuring a fully transparent proxy.

1. Start your proxy server by running `node proxy-server.js`.
2. To test your proxy server, send a request using a tool like `curl` or Postman, with the target URL specified in the headers. For example, using `curl`:
```bash
curl -X GET -H "target-url: http://example.com" http://localhost:8080
```
This request will be forwarded to `http://example.com`, and the response will be returned by your proxy server without any modifications.

To support both HTTP and HTTPS with your transparent proxy server, you'll need to update the code and install an additional package. Follow these steps:

1. Install the `https-proxy-agent` module by running `npm install https-proxy-agent`.
2. Modify the `proxy-server.js` file to include both HTTP and HTTPS support:
```javascript
const http = require('http');
const https = require('https');
const httpProxy = require('http-proxy');
const HttpsProxyAgent = require('https-proxy-agent');

const proxy = httpProxy.createProxyServer({});

const handleRequest = (req, res) => {
  // Obtain the target URL from the request's headers
  const targetUrl = req.headers['target-url'];

  // Check if the target URL is provided
  if (!targetUrl) {
    res.writeHead(400, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('Error: Missing target URL in headers');
    return;
  }

  // Determine the protocol of the target URL
  const targetProtocol = targetUrl.startsWith('https://') ? 'https' : 'http';

  // Set up the appropriate agent for HTTPS requests
  if (targetProtocol === 'https') {
    proxy.options.agent = new HttpsProxyAgent(targetUrl);
  } else {
    proxy.options.agent = null;
  }

  // Forward the request to the target URL
  proxy.web(req, res, { target: targetUrl }, (error) => {
    res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end(`Error: ${error.message}`);
  });
};

const httpServer = http.createServer(handleRequest);

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 8080;

httpServer.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Proxy server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```
This updated code includes the `https` module and `https-proxy-agent`. It determines the target URL's protocol (HTTP or HTTPS) and sets up the appropriate agent accordingly. The `handleRequest` function is used for both HTTP and HTTPS requests.

With these changes, your transparent proxy server will now support both HTTP and HTTPS target URLs. To test, use a tool like `curl` or Postman to send requests with target URLs of both protocols:

For HTTP:
```bash
curl -X GET -H "target-url: http://example.com" http://localhost:8080
```
For HTTPS:
```bash
curl -X GET -H "target-url: https://example.com" http://localhost:8080
```

The `https-proxy-agent` module is a custom HTTP/HTTPS Agent that enables proxy support for Node.js' `https` module. It is designed to work with HTTP/HTTPS requests when you need to send them through a proxy server.

In Node.js, the `http` and `https` modules use an Agent to manage connections and handle request pooling. By default, these modules don't support proxying requests out-of-the-box. This is where the `https-proxy-agent` module comes in.

When you use `https-proxy-agent`, you create an instance of the `HttpsProxyAgent` class by providing the proxy server's URL. This Agent instance can then be passed as an option when making requests with the `https` module, enabling the request to be sent through the specified proxy server.

Here's a simple example of using the `https-proxy-agent` module to make an HTTPS request through a proxy server:
```javascript
const https = require('https');
const HttpsProxyAgent = require('https-proxy-agent');

const proxyUrl = 'http://proxy.example.com:8080'; // Replace with your proxy server's URL
const targetUrl = 'https://example.com'; // Replace with the target HTTPS URL

const proxyAgent = new HttpsProxyAgent(proxyUrl);

const requestOptions = {
  hostname: targetUrl,
  port: 443,
  path: '/',
  method: 'GET',
  agent: proxyAgent,
};

const req = https.request(requestOptions, (res) => {
  console.log(`STATUS: ${res.statusCode}`);
  console.log(`HEADERS: ${JSON.stringify(res.headers)}`);
  res.setEncoding('utf8');
  res.on('data', (chunk) => {
    console.log(`BODY: ${chunk}`);
  });
  res.on('end', () => {
    console.log('No more data in response.');
  });
});

req.on('error', (e) => {
  console.error(`Problem with request: ${e.message}`);
});

req.end();
```
In the proxy server example provided earlier, the `https-proxy-agent` module is used to create an appropriate Agent for forwarding HTTPS requests through the proxy server. This ensures that HTTPS target URLs are supported along with HTTP target URLs.

The "Error: self-signed certificate" message appears when the Node.js process doesn't trust the certificate provided by the server (in this case, Google). This issue can occur if you have custom certificate authorities or self-signed certificates installed on your system.

To bypass this error, you can instruct your proxy server to ignore SSL/TLS certificate validation. Keep in mind that this approach should only be used for testing and debugging purposes, as it may expose you to security risks, such as man-in-the-middle attacks.

Update the `proxy-server.js` file to include the `secure: false` option:
```javascript
const http = require('http');
const https = require('https');
const httpProxy = require('http-proxy');
const HttpsProxyAgent = require('https-proxy-agent');

const proxy = httpProxy.createProxyServer({});

const handleRequest = (req, res) => {
  // Obtain the target URL from the request's headers
  const targetUrl = req.headers['target-url'];

  // Check if the target URL is provided
  if (!targetUrl) {
    res.writeHead(400, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end('Error: Missing target URL in headers');
    return;
  }

  // Determine the protocol of the target URL
  const targetProtocol = targetUrl.startsWith('https://') ? 'https' : 'http';

  // Set up the appropriate agent for HTTPS requests
  if (targetProtocol === 'https') {
    proxy.options.agent = new HttpsProxyAgent(targetUrl);
  } else {
    proxy.options.agent = null;
  }

  // Forward the request to the target URL with the 'secure' option set to false
  proxy.web(req, res, { target: targetUrl, secure: false }, (error) => {
    res.writeHead(500, { 'Content-Type': 'text/plain' });
    res.end(`Error: ${error.message}`);
  });
};

const httpServer = http.createServer(handleRequest);

const PORT = process.env.PORT || 8080;

httpServer.listen(PORT, () => {
  console.log(`Proxy server is running on port ${PORT}`);
});
```
By setting the `secure` option to `false`, the proxy server will not validate the SSL/TLS certificate of the target server.

Now, you should be able to send a request to `https://www.google.com` without encountering the "Error: self-signed certificate" message:
```bash
curl -X GET -H "target-url: https://www.google.com" http://localhost:8080
```
Again, remember that disabling SSL/TLS certificate validation is insecure and should only be done for testing purposes.

