Migrating from session-based authentication to JWT-based authentication in a Next.js application using NextAuth.js with Google OAuth involves a few steps. Here's a high-level outline:

1. Install dependencies:

You should already have `next-auth` installed in your project. If you don't have it, install it with:
```bash
npm install next-auth
```
1. Update your `[...nextauth].js` configuration file:

You'll need to update your NextAuth.js configuration file to use JWTs instead of sessions stored in a database. The configuration file is typically located in your `pages/api/auth` folder and named `[...nextauth].js`.

Here's an example of how you might configure it to use JWTs:
```javascript
import NextAuth from 'next-auth';
import Providers from 'next-auth/providers';
import { JWT } from 'next-auth/jwt';

const options = {
  providers: [
    Providers.Google({
      clientId: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,
      clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET,
    }),
  ],

  // Switch to JWTs
  session: {
    strategy: 'jwt',
  },

  // JWT options
  jwt: {
    secret: process.env.JWT_SECRET,
    encryption: true, // Optional: enable encryption for added security
  },

  callbacks: {
    async jwt(token, user, account, profile, isNewUser) {
      if (user) {
        // Set user data when signing in
        token.id = user.id;
        token.email = user.email;
      }
      return token;
    },

    async session(session, token) {
      // Set user data in session
      session.user = {
        id: token.id,
        email: token.email,
      };
      return session;
    },
  },
};

export default (req, res) => NextAuth(req, res, options);
```
Replace the `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET`, and `JWT_SECRET` values with your actual credentials. Make sure to keep your secrets in a secure environment variable or `.env` file.

1. Update your client-side code:

Instead of using the `useSession` hook from `next-auth/client`, you'll use the `useJwt` hook:
```javascript
import { useJwt } from 'next-auth/client';

function MyComponent() {
  const [jwt, loading] = useJwt();

  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;

  if (!jwt) return <div>You must be signed in to view this content.</div>;

  return <div>Welcome, {jwt.user.email}!</div>;
}

export default MyComponent;
```
1. Remove any code related to session database storage:

Since you're migrating to JWT-based authentication, you can remove any code related to storing sessions in a database.

With these changes, your Next.js application should be using JWT-based authentication with NextAuth.js and Google OAuth.

Migrating from session-based authentication to JWT-based authentication will not cause your users to lose their account information or require them to create new accounts, as long as you maintain the same user identifier (usually the user's email) and provider (Google OAuth in your case).

The user information is typically stored in your database, and as long as the database remains intact, the user information will be preserved. The JWT-based authentication only changes the way the application handles the session, not the user's data in the database.

However, if you're currently storing additional data in the session, you'll need to modify the JWT callback in your `[...nextauth].js` configuration file to include that data in the JWT:
```javascript
callbacks: {
  async jwt(token, user, account, profile, isNewUser) {
    if (user) {
      // Set user data when signing in
      token.id = user.id;
      token.email = user.email;

      // Add any additional data you need
      token.someData = user.someData;
    }
    return token;
  },

  async session(session, token) {
    // Set user data in session
    session.user = {
      id: token.id,
      email: token.email,

      // Add any additional data you need
      someData: token.someData,
    };
    return session;
  },
},
```
When users sign in again after the migration, they will be authenticated using the same Google OAuth provider, and their account information will be retrieved from your database based on their unique identifier (email address in most cases).

In summary, migrating to JWT-based authentication will not cause users to lose their account information or require them to create new accounts, as long as you maintain the same user identifier and provider.

